1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.249
Microservices Architecture.

2
00:00:02.529 --> 00:00:06.966
Chapter 16, from the Distributed Core part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:09.676
At a glance:

4
00:00:09.956 --> 00:00:11.129
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:11.409 --> 00:00:12.412
Reader bands:

6
00:00:12.692 --> 00:00:14.975
B2, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:20.631
Ch 09, Ch 13, Ch 14, Ch 15, What you will build:

8
00:00:20.911 --> 00:00:28.996
The decomposition of the grocery platform into 26 services, and the machinery that keeps it from becoming a distributed monolith.

9
00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:34.460
L1 quantifies what synchronous call chains do to availability and tail latency.

10
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:45.385
L2 runs architecture fitness functions over the service graph: synchronous cycles, chain depth, shared databases and fan-in, before and after redrawing boundaries.

11
00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:52.087
L3 is a modular monolith whose boundaries are enforced by the Java compiler (JPMS).

12
00:00:52.367 --> 00:01:00.239
Reaching into another module's internals, an undeclared dependency and a cycle are all compile errors, shown verbatim.

13
00:01:01.589 --> 00:01:17.909
L4 uses real HTTP on localhost for three things: service discovery with heartbeats versus a static list while an instance dies, API composition with a latency budget, and a strangler-fig migration with shadow diffing and a canary.

14
00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:21.111
Every program was run, and outputs are verbatim.

15
00:01:21.391 --> 00:01:22.373
Reading time:

16
00:01:22.653 --> 00:01:24.445
110 min + code.

17
00:01:25.795 --> 00:01:26.648
Part 1.

18
00:01:26.928 --> 00:01:28.848
Introduction and Why It Matters.

19
00:01:30.098 --> 00:01:31.015
A story:

20
00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:33.749
14 services, one deploy train.

21
00:01:34.029 --> 00:01:42.775
Two years after moving to microservices, the grocery platform had 14 services, and every release still went out together on Thursday night.

22
00:01:43.055 --> 00:01:44.037
What had happened:

23
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:46.877
Three services shared the orders database.

24
00:01:47.157 --> 00:01:51.317
Order, fulfilment and invoicing all read and wrote the same tables.

25
00:01:51.597 --> 00:01:58.530
Adding a column needed three teams in one meeting, and a slow invoicing query locked rows that checkout needed.

26
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.117
Synchronous cycles.

27
00:02:01.397 --> 00:02:05.771
Order called payment to capture, and payment called order back to fetch the amount.

28
00:02:06.051 --> 00:02:08.248
Fulfilment and order called each other.

29
00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:14.757
When payment slowed down, order threads piled up, fulfilment's calls to order timed out, and checkout failed.

30
00:02:15.037 --> 00:02:18.323
A checkout needed 10 services to be up at the same time.

31
00:02:18.603 --> 00:02:30.272
At 99.9 % each, that's roughly 99.0 % for checkout, or about 7 hours of failed checkouts a month, even though every service met its own SLO.

32
00:02:31.622 --> 00:02:36.230
The order-details page called order, payment and tracking one after another.

33
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.310
Tracking had occasional 2-second stalls, which became the page's p99.

34
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:51.233
The architecture had the costs of microservices (network calls, partial failures, distributed data) without the benefit (independent deployability).

35
00:02:51.513 --> 00:02:53.326
It was a distributed monolith.

36
00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.782
The fix wasn't more microservices.

37
00:02:56.062 --> 00:02:57.491
It was better boundaries:

38
00:02:57.771 --> 00:02:59.371
One database per service.

39
00:02:59.651 --> 00:03:04.302
Facts flow as events (Ch 14), not as shared tables or callbacks.

40
00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:13.225
Synchronous calls only where the caller needs an answer now, with no cycles, a shallow depth and timeouts (Ch 18).

41
00:03:13.505 --> 00:03:19.479
Pages that call several services do it in parallel, with a latency budget and partial results.

42
00:03:19.759 --> 00:03:24.495
New capabilities start in a modular monolith with enforced module boundaries.

43
00:03:24.775 --> 00:03:30.919
They're extracted into services only when there's a reason (a team, scaling, a different release cadence).

44
00:03:31.199 --> 00:03:38.089
Legacy paths are replaced piece by piece with a strangler fig: a routing facade, shadow traffic and canaries.

45
00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:40.122
Quote:

46
00:03:40.402 --> 00:03:42.066
The thesis of this chapter.

47
00:03:42.346 --> 00:03:48.021
Microservices are a way to let teams change and deploy parts of a system independently.

48
00:03:48.301 --> 00:03:57.474
Everything else follows from that: boundaries along business capabilities, a database per service, asynchronous facts, few and shallow synchronous calls.

49
00:03:57.754 --> 00:04:08.015
If services can't be deployed independently, or a request needs ten of them to be up, you've built a distributed monolith, and a modular monolith would have served you better.

50
00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.669
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

51
00:04:11.949 --> 00:04:18.477
The 26 services and their bounded contexts are listed in Ch 00 §3.3.

52
00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:23.472
This chapter explains why they're split that way and what rules keep them independent:

53
00:04:24.822 --> 00:04:26.166
Here is a quick table:

54
00:04:26.446 --> 00:04:28.195
Rule, and Example, and Check.

55
00:04:28.475 --> 00:04:31.611
Split by business capability or bounded context.

56
00:04:31.891 --> 00:04:36.819
Example: pricing-service owns prices and GST rates; catalog doesn't.

57
00:04:37.099 --> 00:04:37.782
Check:

58
00:04:38.062 --> 00:04:39.982
Ownership in the service catalog.

59
00:04:40.262 --> 00:04:41.862
One database per service.

60
00:04:42.142 --> 00:04:46.835
Example: invoice-service has invoicedb and builds what it needs from events.

61
00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:47.798
Check:

62
00:04:48.078 --> 00:04:50.830
Fitness function: no shared DB (L2).

63
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.265
Facts as events, commands as calls.

64
00:04:53.545 --> 00:04:58.622
Example: order.order.placed.v1, then fulfilment, invoicing, notifications.

65
00:04:58.902 --> 00:04:59.585
Check:

66
00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:01.785
Async edges in the service graph.

67
00:05:02.065 --> 00:05:04.646
No synchronous cycles; depth at most 4.

68
00:05:04.926 --> 00:05:05.801
Example:

69
00:05:06.081 --> 00:05:10.219
Checkout calls cart, pricing, inventory, payment, order in parallel.

70
00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:11.182
Check:

71
00:05:11.462 --> 00:05:13.083
Fitness function (L2).

72
00:05:13.363 --> 00:05:15.817
Pages compose in parallel with budgets.

73
00:05:16.097 --> 00:05:16.971
Example:

74
00:05:17.251 --> 00:05:21.027
Order details: order + payment required, tracking optional.

75
00:05:21.307 --> 00:05:23.675
Check: p99 SLO (L4 B).

76
00:05:25.025 --> 00:05:27.585
Start modular, extract when justified.

77
00:05:27.865 --> 00:05:28.740
Example:

78
00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:33.095
New subscription capability starts as a module in order-service.

79
00:05:33.375 --> 00:05:34.057
Check:

80
00:05:34.337 --> 00:05:37.559
JPMS or Modulith boundary checks (L3).

81
00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:40.079
Replace legacy with a strangler fig.

82
00:05:40.359 --> 00:05:41.233
Example:

83
00:05:41.513 --> 00:05:45.951
Order history moved off the legacy PHP admin behind the gateway.

84
00:05:46.231 --> 00:05:46.913
Check:

85
00:05:47.193 --> 00:05:50.628
Shadow diff = 0 before cutover (L4 C).

86
00:05:51.978 --> 00:05:53.301
Learning objectives.

87
00:05:53.581 --> 00:05:54.925
Here is a quick table:

88
00:05:55.205 --> 00:05:57.402
Band, and After this chapter you can....

89
00:05:57.682 --> 00:05:58.557
B2:

90
00:05:58.837 --> 00:06:07.157
Explain what microservices are for, decompose by business capability, and state the database-per-service rule and its consequences.

91
00:06:07.437 --> 00:06:08.333
B3:

92
00:06:08.613 --> 00:06:17.701
Design service discovery, externalized configuration and API composition; detect distributed-monolith smells with fitness functions.

93
00:06:17.981 --> 00:06:18.877
B4:

94
00:06:19.157 --> 00:06:28.799
Choose between a modular monolith and microservices, plan a strangler-fig migration, and align service boundaries with team topology (Conway's law).

95
00:06:30.149 --> 00:06:31.024
Part 2.

96
00:06:31.304 --> 00:06:33.181
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

97
00:06:34.431 --> 00:06:37.482
Intuition: one big kitchen versus a food court.

98
00:06:37.762 --> 00:06:43.351
A big restaurant kitchen (a monolith) has one head chef, one menu and one set of equipment.

99
00:06:43.631 --> 00:06:49.029
Coordination is easy (shout across the room), and changing the menu is one decision.

100
00:06:49.309 --> 00:06:54.578
But every new dish means the whole kitchen retrains, and a fire at the grill stops everything.

101
00:06:54.858 --> 00:07:01.663
A food court (microservices) is independent stalls, each with its own staff, menu, suppliers and hours.

102
00:07:01.943 --> 00:07:05.847
The dosa stall can change its recipe without asking the pizza stall.

103
00:07:06.127 --> 00:07:15.151
But a combo meal needs several stalls to coordinate, the customer walks between counters, and if the drinks stall is closed, the combo is incomplete.

104
00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:23.947
A food court where every stall shares one fridge and must ask the next stall for every ingredient is the distributed monolith.

105
00:07:24.227 --> 00:07:26.851
You get all the walking and none of the independence.

106
00:07:27.131 --> 00:07:36.539
A big kitchen with clearly separated stations, each with its own shelf and a rule that nobody takes from another station's shelf, is the modular monolith.

107
00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:43.901
It keeps the easy coordination and most of the independence, and it can become a food court later, station by station.

108
00:07:45.251 --> 00:07:47.811
What microservices are, and what they're for.

109
00:07:48.091 --> 00:07:58.033
A microservice is an independently deployable unit that owns a business capability and its data, and communicates through well-defined APIs and events.

110
00:07:58.313 --> 00:08:07.529
The defining property is independent deployability: a team can change, test and release its service without coordinating a release with other teams.

111
00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:10.223
Here is a quick table:

112
00:08:10.503 --> 00:08:11.996
Benefit, and Only if....

113
00:08:12.276 --> 00:08:14.537
Team autonomy and parallel delivery:

114
00:08:14.817 --> 00:08:18.465
Boundaries match teams (Conway's law), contracts are versioned.

115
00:08:18.745 --> 00:08:20.004
Independent scaling:

116
00:08:20.284 --> 00:08:23.847
Services have different load shapes (search versus invoicing).

117
00:08:24.127 --> 00:08:25.364
Fault isolation:

118
00:08:25.644 --> 00:08:30.145
Calls have timeouts and fallbacks, and dependencies are few (Ch 18).

119
00:08:30.425 --> 00:08:31.641
Technology fit:

120
00:08:31.921 --> 00:08:35.527
The platform can support more than one stack (usually: don't).

121
00:08:35.807 --> 00:08:37.151
Smaller codebases:

122
00:08:37.431 --> 00:08:39.500
Boundaries are stable and cohesive.

123
00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:42.194
Here is a quick table:

124
00:08:42.474 --> 00:08:43.882
Cost, and Consequence.

125
00:08:44.162 --> 00:08:46.317
Network calls instead of method calls:

126
00:08:46.597 --> 00:08:49.882
Latency, partial failure, retries, serialization.

127
00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:51.442
Distributed data:

128
00:08:51.722 --> 00:09:00.298
No joins or transactions across services: sagas (Ch 13), events (Ch 14), CQRS (Ch 15).

129
00:09:00.578 --> 00:09:01.602
Operations:

130
00:09:01.882 --> 00:09:07.685
N deployments, dashboards, on-calls, CVEs, pipelines (Ch 24, 26).

131
00:09:07.965 --> 00:09:08.797
Testing:

132
00:09:09.077 --> 00:09:12.917
Contracts, environments, end-to-end cost (Ch 29).

133
00:09:13.197 --> 00:09:14.455
Availability math:

134
00:09:14.735 --> 00:09:17.978
Every synchronous dependency multiplies in (L1).

135
00:09:19.328 --> 00:09:21.461
L1 puts numbers on the last line:

136
00:09:21.741 --> 00:09:23.405
Availability multiplies:

137
00:09:23.685 --> 00:09:33.861
5 synchronous services in series at 99.9 % each gives 99.50 % (216 min per month of failures).

138
00:09:34.141 --> 00:09:36.659
12 gives 98.81 %.

139
00:09:36.939 --> 00:09:39.392
Latency adds up when calls are sequential:

140
00:09:39.672 --> 00:09:44.259
8 calls go from p50 20 ms to 178 ms.

141
00:09:44.539 --> 00:09:48.507
Parallel calls take the maximum instead, but the tail still grows:

142
00:09:48.787 --> 00:09:55.549
26 parallel calls give 23 % of page loads that hit at least one dependency's p99.

143
00:09:56.899 --> 00:10:00.590
Decomposition: business capabilities and bounded contexts.

144
00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.451
By business capability: what the business does.

145
00:10:03.731 --> 00:10:12.926
Examples: catalog management, pricing, promotions, ordering, fulfilment, delivery dispatch, payments, settlements, invoicing, customer support.

146
00:10:13.206 --> 00:10:19.201
By bounded context (DDD, Ch 33): where a model and its language are consistent.

147
00:10:19.481 --> 00:10:22.553
Product in the catalog has descriptions and images.

148
00:10:22.833 --> 00:10:26.779
In pricing, it has MRP, selling price and GST slab.

149
00:10:27.059 --> 00:10:30.281
In inventory, it's a SKU with stock per dark store.

150
00:10:31.631 --> 00:10:34.319
In fulfilment, it's a pick location and weight.

151
00:10:34.599 --> 00:10:38.481
These are four models of one real-world thing, in four contexts.

152
00:10:38.761 --> 00:10:43.241
Forcing one shared Product model across them is how the monolith got coupled.

153
00:10:43.521 --> 00:10:45.079
Heuristics for boundaries:

154
00:10:46.429 --> 00:10:47.773
Here is a quick table:

155
00:10:48.053 --> 00:10:50.506
Heuristic, and Good sign, and Bad sign.

156
00:10:50.786 --> 00:10:52.919
Change together, then belong together.

157
00:10:53.199 --> 00:10:54.053
Good sign:

158
00:10:54.333 --> 00:10:56.146
A feature touches one service.

159
00:10:56.426 --> 00:10:57.407
Bad sign:

160
00:10:57.687 --> 00:10:59.863
Every feature touches 3 services.

161
00:11:00.143 --> 00:11:01.295
Data ownership.

162
00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:02.429
Good sign:

163
00:11:02.709 --> 00:11:04.415
Each table has one writer.

164
00:11:04.695 --> 00:11:05.677
Bad sign:

165
00:11:05.957 --> 00:11:08.261
Several services write the same table.

166
00:11:08.541 --> 00:11:09.607
Transactions.

167
00:11:09.887 --> 00:11:10.741
Good sign:

168
00:11:11.021 --> 00:11:13.709
Invariants are enforced inside one service.

169
00:11:13.989 --> 00:11:14.970
Bad sign:

170
00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.661
Invariants need cross-service transactions.

171
00:11:17.941 --> 00:11:18.837
Language.

172
00:11:19.117 --> 00:11:19.970
Good sign:

173
00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.362
Terms mean one thing inside the service.

174
00:11:22.642 --> 00:11:23.623
Bad sign:

175
00:11:23.903 --> 00:11:25.333
Order means three things.

176
00:11:25.613 --> 00:11:26.338
Team.

177
00:11:26.618 --> 00:11:27.471
Good sign:

178
00:11:27.751 --> 00:11:29.458
One team owns it end to end.

179
00:11:29.738 --> 00:11:30.719
Bad sign:

180
00:11:30.999 --> 00:11:32.813
Several teams commit to it weekly.

181
00:11:33.093 --> 00:11:34.031
Chattiness.

182
00:11:34.311 --> 00:11:35.165
Good sign:

183
00:11:35.445 --> 00:11:37.301
A few coarse calls per use case.

184
00:11:37.581 --> 00:11:38.562
Bad sign:

185
00:11:38.842 --> 00:11:40.549
Dozens of fine-grained calls.

186
00:11:41.899 --> 00:11:44.373
Granularity: start coarser than you think.

187
00:11:44.653 --> 00:11:49.752
Splitting a service later is easier than merging services that share nothing but pain.

188
00:11:50.032 --> 00:11:55.472
The grocery platform's 26 services map to about 8 teams (Ch 37).

189
00:11:55.752 --> 00:11:59.229
A team owning 3 to 4 cohesive services is normal.

190
00:11:59.509 --> 00:12:03.541
A service per entity (address-service, gst-rate-service) isn't.

191
00:12:04.891 --> 00:12:06.321
Database per service.

192
00:12:06.601 --> 00:12:08.265
Each service owns its data.

193
00:12:08.545 --> 00:12:12.726
Other services access it only through the owner's API or events.

194
00:12:13.006 --> 00:12:15.075
They never read or write its tables.

195
00:12:15.355 --> 00:12:26.001
Why: schema changes stay local, the owner enforces invariants, the owner chooses the right storage (Ch 06), and a noisy neighbour can't lock your rows.

196
00:12:26.281 --> 00:12:36.563
Private tables on a shared server is an acceptable start: separate schemas and users on one PostgreSQL cluster, as long as grants enforce the separation.

197
00:12:36.843 --> 00:12:39.019
It's the shared tables that are the problem.

198
00:12:39.299 --> 00:12:41.155
Consequences, and their patterns:

199
00:12:42.505 --> 00:12:51.572
Cross-service queries, then API composition (§2.8) or a CQRS read model built from events (Ch 15).

200
00:12:51.852 --> 00:12:56.311
Cross-service transactions, then sagas + outbox (Ch 13).

201
00:12:56.591 --> 00:13:07.663
Reference data (pincodes, GST rates), then owned by one service, replicated to others via event-carried state transfer (Ch 15), and cached with versions.

202
00:13:07.943 --> 00:13:14.812
Reporting, then CDC into the warehouse (Ch 21), never live joins across service databases.

203
00:13:16.162 --> 00:13:27.895
L2's before graph had two shared databases (ordersdb used by order, fulfilment and invoice; catalogdb used by catalog and pricing), and the fitness check failed on them.

204
00:13:29.245 --> 00:13:30.632
Communication styles.

205
00:13:30.912 --> 00:13:32.256
Here is a quick table:

206
00:13:32.536 --> 00:13:34.755
Style, and Use for, and Watch out for.

207
00:13:35.035 --> 00:13:40.773
Synchronous request or response (REST, gRPC; Ch 04, 17).

208
00:13:41.053 --> 00:13:41.907
Use for:

209
00:13:42.187 --> 00:13:47.712
Queries and commands where the caller needs the answer now (price, stock check, payment result).

210
00:13:47.992 --> 00:13:49.037
Watch out for:

211
00:13:49.317 --> 00:13:53.115
Availability multiplication, cascading latency, cycles.

212
00:13:53.395 --> 00:13:55.656
Asynchronous events (Ch 14).

213
00:13:55.936 --> 00:13:56.789
Use for:

214
00:13:57.069 --> 00:14:00.077
Facts others react to (order placed, price changed).

215
00:14:00.357 --> 00:14:01.403
Watch out for:

216
00:14:01.683 --> 00:14:04.456
Eventual consistency, duplicates, ordering.

217
00:14:04.736 --> 00:14:07.061
Asynchronous commands or jobs (queues).

218
00:14:07.341 --> 00:14:08.195
Use for:

219
00:14:08.475 --> 00:14:12.037
Work for one owner, later (send SMS, generate invoice).

220
00:14:12.317 --> 00:14:13.363
Watch out for:

221
00:14:13.643 --> 00:14:15.072
Retries, DLQs.

222
00:14:16.422 --> 00:14:18.065
Rules the platform follows:

223
00:14:18.345 --> 00:14:19.774
No synchronous cycles.

224
00:14:20.054 --> 00:14:26.582
A calls B, and B calls A is a single deployable unit in disguise, and a deadlock or cascade risk.

225
00:14:26.862 --> 00:14:35.801
L2 found two cycles in the before graph (order ↔ payment ↔ fulfilment, catalog ↔ inventory).

226
00:14:36.081 --> 00:14:40.881
Shallow synchronous depth: gateway, then aggregator, then leaf ( at most 4 hops).

227
00:14:41.161 --> 00:14:48.841
The before graph had a 6-hop chain (gateway, then checkout, then order, then invoice, then pricing, then catalog, then inventory).

228
00:14:50.191 --> 00:14:51.983
Invert dependencies with events.

229
00:14:52.263 --> 00:14:59.473
Instead of payment calling order back, payment publishes payment.payment.captured.v1, and the order saga reacts.

230
00:14:59.753 --> 00:15:06.239
Every call has a timeout, and retries only on idempotent operations with backoff (Ch 18).

231
00:15:06.519 --> 00:15:08.737
Keep the synchronous blast radius small:

232
00:15:09.017 --> 00:15:14.308
L2's after graph cut the services a checkout needs synchronously from 10 to 6.

233
00:15:15.658 --> 00:15:18.047
Service discovery and load balancing.

234
00:15:18.327 --> 00:15:24.301
Instances come and go (autoscaling, deploys, crashes), so callers need the current, healthy set.

235
00:15:25.651 --> 00:15:26.995
Here is a quick table:

236
00:15:27.275 --> 00:15:29.195
Approach, and How, and Examples.

237
00:15:29.475 --> 00:15:32.547
Platform DNS + L4 or L7 LB.

238
00:15:32.827 --> 00:15:33.488
How:

239
00:15:33.768 --> 00:15:39.315
A stable name resolves to a virtual IP or load balancer; the platform tracks health.

240
00:15:39.595 --> 00:15:40.555
Examples:

241
00:15:40.835 --> 00:15:43.224
Kubernetes Services, cloud LBs.

242
00:15:43.504 --> 00:15:44.955
Client-side discovery.

243
00:15:45.235 --> 00:15:45.896
How:

244
00:15:46.176 --> 00:15:50.101
The client asks a registry for instances and balances itself.

245
00:15:50.381 --> 00:15:51.341
Examples:

246
00:15:51.621 --> 00:15:54.565
Eureka + Spring Cloud LoadBalancer, Consul.

247
00:15:54.845 --> 00:15:55.869
Service mesh.

248
00:15:56.149 --> 00:15:56.811
How:

249
00:15:57.091 --> 00:16:03.427
A sidecar or ambient proxy does discovery, retries, mTLS and outlier detection.

250
00:16:03.707 --> 00:16:04.667
Examples:

251
00:16:04.947 --> 00:16:06.483
Istio, Linkerd, Cilium.

252
00:16:07.833 --> 00:16:10.457
Whatever the mechanism, three behaviours matter:

253
00:16:10.737 --> 00:16:18.139
Dead instances are removed quickly: health checks, heartbeats with a TTL, readiness probes (Ch 26).

254
00:16:18.419 --> 00:16:21.982
Callers retry on another instance for idempotent requests.

255
00:16:22.262 --> 00:16:24.481
Clients don't cache instance lists forever.

256
00:16:24.761 --> 00:16:31.033
L4 A made 3,000 calls to three catalog instances, and one crashed after call 500:

257
00:16:32.383 --> 00:16:38.228
A static instance list failed 833 calls (every third call went to the dead instance).

258
00:16:38.508 --> 00:16:43.969
A registry with a 300 ms heartbeat TTL plus one retry failed 0.

259
00:16:44.249 --> 00:16:48.943
On Kubernetes, the Service or Endpoints mechanism does the registry part.

260
00:16:49.223 --> 00:16:58.289
Readiness probes must fail before an instance stops accepting traffic (graceful shutdown), or the same failures appear during every deploy.

261
00:16:59.639 --> 00:17:01.474
Externalized configuration.

262
00:17:01.754 --> 00:17:11.226
Configuration (URLs, timeouts, feature flags, limits) lives outside the artifact, so one build runs in every environment (12-factor).

263
00:17:11.506 --> 00:17:25.074
Sources: environment variables and ConfigMaps (Kubernetes), Spring Cloud Config, Consul or etcd (Ch 12), and a secrets manager for credentials (Vault, cloud KMS; Ch 23).

264
00:17:25.354 --> 00:17:35.914
Dynamic config and flags (rate limits, kill switches) are versioned and audited, with safe defaults when the config store is unreachable, and validated before rollout.

265
00:17:36.194 --> 00:17:37.901
A config push is a deployment.

266
00:17:39.251 --> 00:17:43.197
Avoid a config service that every request depends on synchronously.

267
00:17:43.477 --> 00:17:47.061
Load it at startup and on change (watch), then serve from memory.

268
00:17:48.411 --> 00:17:50.694
API composition and the gateway.

269
00:17:50.974 --> 00:17:53.897
Many screens need data from several services.

270
00:17:54.177 --> 00:17:55.051
Options:

271
00:17:55.331 --> 00:18:03.438
Client-side composition (the app calls several APIs): chatty over mobile networks, and it exposes internal APIs.

272
00:18:03.718 --> 00:18:16.667
API composition in a gateway or BFF (backend-for-frontend): one call from the app, and the BFF calls services in parallel, with a latency budget and partial results for optional parts.

273
00:18:16.947 --> 00:18:25.630
A CQRS read model (Ch 15) when the combined view is requested often or needs filtering or sorting across services.

274
00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:27.510
It's pre-joined by events.

275
