375
00:22:46.793 --> 00:22:47.518
Hard.

376
00:22:47.798 --> 00:22:49.142
Modular monolith:

377
00:22:49.422 --> 00:22:51.129
A module becomes a service.

378
00:22:52.479 --> 00:22:56.831
L3 enforces the boundaries with the Java Platform Module System.

379
00:22:57.111 --> 00:23:11.063
Each module exports only its api package. grocery.order requires grocery.catalog and grocery.shared, but not grocery.inventory, and it reaches inventory only by publishing OrderPlaced.

380
00:23:11.343 --> 00:23:15.567
Then three violations are attempted, and the compiler rejects each one:

381
00:23:15.847 --> 00:23:24.380
Importing com.grocery.catalog.internal.ProductRepository, then package com.grocery.catalog.internal is not visible.

382
00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:31.380
Using inventory without declaring it, then package com.grocery.inventory.api is not visible.

383
00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:39.258
Adding requires grocery.order to catalog, then cyclic dependence involving grocery.catalog.

384
00:23:39.538 --> 00:23:45.981
In Spring Boot, Spring Modulith gives the same idea with packages instead of JPMS modules:

385
00:23:46.261 --> 00:23:51.807
ApplicationModules.of(App.class).verify() fails the build on illegal dependencies.

386
00:23:52.087 --> 00:24:03.138
It also offers @ApplicationModuleListener for transactional in-process events with an event publication registry (outbox semantics), and documentation of the module graph.

387
00:24:04.488 --> 00:24:22.237
When to extract a module into a service: a different team owns it and needs its own release cadence, its load or scaling profile differs sharply, it needs different technology or isolation (PCI scope for payments), or failure isolation justifies the network hop.

388
00:24:22.517 --> 00:24:24.245
It feels big isn't on the list.

389
00:24:25.595 --> 00:24:27.494
Migrating: the strangler fig.

390
00:24:27.774 --> 00:24:33.406
Martin Fowler's strangler fig replaces a legacy system gradually, not in a big-bang rewrite:

391
00:24:33.686 --> 00:24:37.633
Put a facade in front (the API gateway or a routing proxy).

392
00:24:37.913 --> 00:24:39.534
All traffic flows through it.

393
00:24:39.814 --> 00:24:46.833
Pick a slice: one capability, and ideally one that's read-heavy first (order history), not the most entangled.

394
00:24:47.113 --> 00:24:53.598
Build the new service, and feed it data via CDC or events from the legacy DB (Ch 13).

395
00:24:54.948 --> 00:24:57.807
Shadow: the facade sends real requests to both.

396
00:24:58.087 --> 00:25:00.903
The legacy answers, and the responses are diffed.

397
00:25:01.183 --> 00:25:02.185
L4 C:

398
00:25:02.465 --> 00:25:10.508
468 mismatches in 2,000 requests, all for large baskets, a rounding bug found before any user saw it.

399
00:25:10.788 --> 00:25:13.860
Canary: after the fix, route a small share (L4:

400
00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:19.644
214 of 2,000) to the new service while still diffing (0 mismatches).

401
00:25:19.924 --> 00:25:20.948
Then ramp up.

402
00:25:21.228 --> 00:25:23.447
Cut over and retire the legacy path.

403
00:25:23.727 --> 00:25:25.519
Delete the dead code and tables.

404
00:25:25.799 --> 00:25:27.313
Repeat with the next slice.

405
00:25:27.593 --> 00:25:37.663
Writes are harder: move the write path only after reads are stable, and use CDC in the reverse direction during the transition if the legacy still needs the data.

406
00:25:39.013 --> 00:25:48.271
Anti-corruption layer (ACL): translate the legacy's model to the new context's model at the boundary, so legacy concepts don't leak into the new service.

407
00:25:49.621 --> 00:25:51.307
Microservices and teams.

408
00:25:51.587 --> 00:25:57.240
Conway's law: systems mirror the communication structure of the organizations that build them.

409
00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:01.979
The inverse Conway manoeuvre designs teams to get the architecture you want.

410
00:26:02.259 --> 00:26:07.635
Team Topologies (Skelton and Pais): stream-aligned teams own services end to end.

411
00:26:07.915 --> 00:26:13.952
A platform team provides paved roads (CI or CD, observability, the service template).

412
00:26:14.232 --> 00:26:19.373
Enabling teams coach, and complicated-subsystem teams own things like search ranking.

413
00:26:19.653 --> 00:26:21.787
Cognitive load is the real limit.

414
00:26:22.067 --> 00:26:28.424
If one team owns 9 services and 4 of them page at night, the boundaries (or the team count) are wrong.

415
00:26:29.774 --> 00:26:46.286
A service template (golden path) is how 26 services stay consistent: the four-layer structure, logging, tracing, health, config, security, the outbox or inbox library and the CI pipeline, generated from one template (Ch 37).

