00:49:23.174 --> 00:49:31.089
With heartbeats (100 ms), a 300 ms TTL and one retry on another instance for this idempotent GET:

717
00:49:31.369 --> 00:49:32.521
0 failures.

718
00:49:32.801 --> 00:49:41.974
On Kubernetes, the endpoints controller and readiness probes play the registry's role, and the retry belongs in the client or mesh (Ch 18).

719
00:49:42.254 --> 00:49:42.937
B.

720
00:49:43.217 --> 00:49:54.481
Parallel calls fixed the median (79, then 31 ms) but not the p99 (~2 s), because the slow optional call still gated the response.

721
00:49:54.761 --> 00:50:04.723
A 250 ms budget for tracking made the p99 251 ms, with 10 of 500 pages showing tracking updating.

722
00:50:06.073 --> 00:50:06.756
C.

723
00:50:07.036 --> 00:50:16.828
Shadowing found 468 mismatches in 2,000 requests, all for baskets where the new service rounded differently, before any customer saw them.

724
00:50:17.108 --> 00:50:27.220
After the fix, a 10 % canary (214 requests) plus continued diffing showed 0 mismatches, so the route can go to 100 %.

725
00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:30.337
Spring equivalents (not compiled here, VERIFY):

726
00:50:30.617 --> 00:50:31.513
Discovery:

727
00:50:31.793 --> 00:50:40.945
Kubernetes Services + spring.cloud.kubernetes.discovery, or @LoadBalanced RestClient.Builder with Spring Cloud LoadBalancer.

728
00:50:41.225 --> 00:50:42.164
Composition:

729
00:50:42.444 --> 00:50:49.804
StructuredTaskScope (preview in 21, check your JDK) or CompletableFuture.allOf with per-call timeouts.

730
00:50:51.154 --> 00:50:52.349
Strangler routing:

731
00:50:52.629 --> 00:51:05.535
Spring Cloud Gateway predicates (Path=/orders//history, Weight=history, 10), with shadow traffic via a gateway filter that mirrors requests asynchronously and logs diffs.

732
00:51:06.885 --> 00:51:09.019
What changed from L1 to L4.

733
00:51:09.299 --> 00:51:10.643
Here is a quick table:

734
00:51:10.923 --> 00:51:14.379
Concern, and L1, and L2, and L3, and L4.

735
00:51:14.659 --> 00:51:15.725
Availability.

736
00:51:16.005 --> 00:51:16.837
L1:

737
00:51:17.117 --> 00:51:20.616
12 serial deps, then 98.81 %.

738
00:51:20.896 --> 00:51:21.707
L2:

739
00:51:21.987 --> 00:51:23.800
Checkout deps 10, then 6.

740
00:51:24.080 --> 00:51:24.933
L3:

741
00:51:25.213 --> 00:51:26.941
One process, no network.

742
00:51:27.221 --> 00:51:28.096
L4:

743
00:51:28.376 --> 00:51:29.400
Dead instance:

744
00:51:29.680 --> 00:51:32.475
833, then 0 failed calls.

745
00:51:32.755 --> 00:51:33.672
Latency.

746
00:51:33.952 --> 00:51:34.784
L1:

747
00:51:35.064 --> 00:51:38.520
Serial 8 calls p50 178 ms.

748
00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:39.611
L2:

749
00:51:39.891 --> 00:51:41.192
Depth 6, then 2.

750
00:51:41.472 --> 00:51:42.325
L3:

751
00:51:42.605 --> 00:51:43.608
Method calls.

752
00:51:43.888 --> 00:51:44.763
L4:

753
00:51:45.043 --> 00:51:49.949
Page p99 2,054, then 251 ms.

754
00:51:50.229 --> 00:51:50.997
Coupling.

755
00:51:51.277 --> 00:51:52.088
L2:

756
00:51:52.368 --> 00:51:54.971
2 cycles, 2 shared DBs, then 0.

757
00:51:55.251 --> 00:51:56.104
L3:

758
00:51:56.384 --> 00:51:58.304
Violations are compile errors.

759
00:51:58.584 --> 00:51:59.459
L4:

760
00:51:59.739 --> 00:52:02.576
Facade decouples clients from where logic lives.

761
00:52:02.856 --> 00:52:03.923
Change safety.

762
00:52:04.203 --> 00:52:05.013
L2:

763
00:52:05.293 --> 00:52:06.744
Fitness checks in CI.

764
00:52:07.024 --> 00:52:07.877
L3:

765
00:52:08.157 --> 00:52:10.184
Boundaries enforced by javac.

766
00:52:10.464 --> 00:52:11.339
L4:

767
00:52:11.619 --> 00:52:15.373
Shadow diff (468, then 0) before cutover.

768
00:52:16.723 --> 00:52:17.662
Part 6.

769
00:52:17.942 --> 00:52:19.222
Production Concerns.

770
00:52:20.472 --> 00:52:21.624
Failure modes.

771
00:52:21.904 --> 00:52:23.248
Here is a quick table:

772
00:52:23.528 --> 00:52:27.411
Failure, and Detection signal, and Blast radius, and Mitigation.

773
00:52:27.691 --> 00:52:30.037
Cascading failure through synchronous chains.

774
00:52:30.317 --> 00:52:31.512
Detection signal:

775
00:52:31.792 --> 00:52:34.779
Latency or error spikes propagating up the graph.

776
00:52:35.059 --> 00:52:36.275
Blast radius:

777
00:52:36.555 --> 00:52:38.325
Every caller of the slow service.

778
00:52:38.605 --> 00:52:39.608
Mitigation:

779
00:52:39.888 --> 00:52:44.517
Timeouts, bulkheads, circuit breakers (Ch 18), shallow graphs (L2).

780
00:52:44.797 --> 00:52:45.971
Synchronous cycle.

781
00:52:46.251 --> 00:52:47.445
Detection signal:

782
00:52:47.725 --> 00:52:49.965
Thread pools exhausted on both sides.

783
00:52:50.245 --> 00:52:51.461
Blast radius:

784
00:52:51.741 --> 00:52:53.747
Both services, then their callers.

785
00:52:54.027 --> 00:52:55.029
Mitigation:

786
00:52:55.309 --> 00:52:57.187
Replace one direction with events.

787
00:52:57.467 --> 00:52:59.152
Shared database contention.

788
00:52:59.432 --> 00:53:00.627
Detection signal:

789
00:53:00.907 --> 00:53:03.125
Lock waits from another service's queries.

790
00:53:03.405 --> 00:53:04.621
Blast radius:

791
00:53:04.901 --> 00:53:06.565
Services sharing the DB.

792
00:53:06.845 --> 00:53:07.848
Mitigation:

793
00:53:08.128 --> 00:53:10.347
Database per service; read models.

794
00:53:10.627 --> 00:53:12.739
Dead instance still receiving traffic.

795
00:53:13.019 --> 00:53:14.213
Detection signal:

796
00:53:14.493 --> 00:53:16.264
Connection errors from one pod.

797
00:53:16.544 --> 00:53:17.760
Blast radius:

798
00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:20.216
1 or N of requests (L4 A:

799
00:53:20.496 --> 00:53:23.483
833 of 2,500).

800
00:53:23.763 --> 00:53:24.765
Mitigation:

801
00:53:25.045 --> 00:53:30.187
Readiness probes, heartbeat TTL, retry on another instance, graceful shutdown.

802
00:53:31.537 --> 00:53:33.265
Slow optional dependency.

803
00:53:33.545 --> 00:53:34.739
Detection signal:

804
00:53:35.019 --> 00:53:37.835
Page p99 tracks that dependency.

805
00:53:38.115 --> 00:53:39.331
Blast radius:

806
00:53:39.611 --> 00:53:41.766
Whole page latency (L4 B:

807
00:53:42.046 --> 00:53:42.899
2 s).

808
00:53:43.179 --> 00:53:44.182
Mitigation:

809
00:53:44.462 --> 00:53:46.297
Budgets, partial responses.

810
00:53:46.577 --> 00:53:48.433
Version skew between services.

811
00:53:48.713 --> 00:53:49.907
Detection signal:

812
00:53:50.187 --> 00:53:53.579
4xx or 5xx after one side deploys.

813
00:53:53.859 --> 00:53:55.075
Blast radius:

814
00:53:55.355 --> 00:53:56.955
Calls between those versions.

815
00:53:57.235 --> 00:53:58.238
Mitigation:

816
00:53:58.518 --> 00:54:05.259
Backward-compatible contracts, consumer-driven contract tests (Ch 29), expand or contract.

817
00:54:05.539 --> 00:54:07.587
Config push breaks many services.

818
00:54:07.867 --> 00:54:09.062
Detection signal:

819
00:54:09.342 --> 00:54:11.454
Errors right after a config change.

820
00:54:11.734 --> 00:54:12.950
Blast radius:

821
00:54:13.230 --> 00:54:15.001
All consumers of that config.

822
00:54:15.281 --> 00:54:16.283
Mitigation:

823
00:54:16.563 --> 00:54:19.742
Validate, stage, and roll back config like code.

824
00:54:20.022 --> 00:54:22.753
Legacy or new divergence during migration.

825
00:54:23.033 --> 00:54:24.227
Detection signal:

826
00:54:24.507 --> 00:54:25.979
Shadow diff mismatches.

827
00:54:26.259 --> 00:54:27.475
Blast radius:

828
00:54:27.755 --> 00:54:29.505
Users if cut over too early.

829
00:54:29.785 --> 00:54:30.787
Mitigation:

830
00:54:31.067 --> 00:54:35.270
Shadow, then canary, then ramp, with diff = 0 as the gate.

831
00:54:36.620 --> 00:54:37.708
Observability.

832
00:54:37.988 --> 00:54:45.113
Distributed tracing on every request (W3C trace context through HTTP and Kafka headers).

833
00:54:45.393 --> 00:54:50.279
It's the only way to see the real call graph and where time goes (Ch 24).

834
00:54:50.559 --> 00:54:58.473
RED metrics per service and per dependency (rate, errors, duration), with dashboards generated by the service template.

835
00:54:58.753 --> 00:55:02.487
Dependency graph from traces versus the declared catalog.

836
00:55:02.767 --> 00:55:07.076
Alert on new undeclared synchronous edges (they're how cycles creep back).

837
00:55:07.356 --> 00:55:15.612
Deployment metrics: deploys per service, lead time, change failure rate, % multi-service releases (the release train smell).

838
00:55:16.962 --> 00:55:19.927
BFF partial-response rate per optional field.

839
00:55:20.207 --> 00:55:23.343
A rising rate means a dependency is degrading.

840
00:55:24.693 --> 00:55:25.632
Security.

841
00:55:25.912 --> 00:55:36.664
Zero trust between services: mTLS (mesh or library), service identity (SPIFFE), and authorization per endpoint (which service may call which API).

842
00:55:36.944 --> 00:55:40.677
Don't rely on a trusted internal network (Ch 23).

843
00:55:40.957 --> 00:55:48.872
Propagate end-user context as a token (a JWT exchanged for downstream scopes), not as trusted headers.

844
00:55:49.152 --> 00:55:55.680
A database per service also means credentials per service, least privilege, and no shared admin users.

845
00:55:55.960 --> 00:56:05.219
The gateway or BFF is the edge: authentication, rate limiting, input validation, and never exposing internal APIs directly.

846
00:56:06.569 --> 00:56:08.233
Performance and tuning knobs.

847
00:56:08.513 --> 00:56:09.857
Here is a quick table:

848
00:56:10.137 --> 00:56:12.121
Knob, and Default here, and Effect.

849
00:56:12.401 --> 00:56:13.830
Synchronous depth limit.

850
00:56:14.110 --> 00:56:16.094
Default here: at most 4 hops.

851
00:56:16.374 --> 00:56:17.163
Effect:

852
00:56:17.443 --> 00:56:19.257
Latency and availability.

853
00:56:19.537 --> 00:56:20.689
Per-call timeout.

854
00:56:20.969 --> 00:56:25.278
Default here: p99.9 of the dependency + margin.

855
00:56:25.558 --> 00:56:26.347
Effect:

856
00:56:26.627 --> 00:56:28.953
Tail latency versus false failures.

857
00:56:29.233 --> 00:56:31.430
Optional-part budget in BFFs.

858
00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:32.627
Default here:

859
00:56:32.907 --> 00:56:34.422
250 ms.

860
00:56:34.702 --> 00:56:35.491
Effect:

861
00:56:35.771 --> 00:56:38.417
Page p99 versus completeness.

862
00:56:38.697 --> 00:56:41.918
Heartbeat interval or TTL (or readiness period).

863
00:56:42.198 --> 00:56:43.115
Default here:

864
00:56:43.395 --> 00:56:48.750
100 ms or 300 ms in the lab; 5 s or 15 s typical.

865
00:56:49.030 --> 00:56:49.819
Effect:

866
00:56:50.099 --> 00:56:51.934
Eviction speed versus flapping.

867
00:56:52.214 --> 00:56:54.070
Connection pools per dependency.

868
00:56:54.350 --> 00:56:55.267
Default here:

869
00:56:55.547 --> 00:56:57.595
Sized per dependency (bulkheads).

870
00:56:57.875 --> 00:56:58.665
Effect:

871
00:56:58.945 --> 00:57:01.099
Isolation versus resource use.

872
00:57:02.449 --> 00:57:03.516
Payload shape.

873
00:57:03.796 --> 00:57:04.713
Default here:

874
00:57:04.993 --> 00:57:06.295
Coarse-grained, paged.

875
00:57:06.575 --> 00:57:07.364
Effect:

876
00:57:07.644 --> 00:57:09.393
Fewer calls; less chattiness.

877
00:57:10.743 --> 00:57:11.469
Cost.

878
00:57:11.749 --> 00:57:22.522
Each service has a fixed cost: pods (at least 2 to 3 for HA), a database, pipelines, dashboards, alerts, on-call, dependency upgrades and CVE patching.

879
00:57:22.802 --> 00:57:27.133
26 services times that fixed cost is real money and real toil.

880
00:57:27.413 --> 00:57:30.677
The platform team's paved road is what keeps it affordable.

881
00:57:30.957 --> 00:57:36.119
Network calls cost CPU (serialization) and cloud money (cross-AZ traffic).

882
00:57:36.399 --> 00:57:38.298
Chatty designs show up on the bill.

883
00:57:38.578 --> 00:57:41.479
Modular monoliths are cheaper per capability.

884
00:57:41.759 --> 00:57:47.754
Extract a service when its benefit (autonomy, scaling, isolation) is worth that fixed cost.

885
00:57:49.104 --> 00:57:50.043
Part 7.

886
00:57:50.323 --> 00:57:52.029
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

887
00:57:53.279 --> 00:57:53.919
1.

888
00:57:54.199 --> 00:57:56.162
Shared database between services.

889
00:57:56.442 --> 00:57:57.253
Symptom:

890
00:57:57.533 --> 00:58:03.719
Schema changes need several teams, and one service's query locks another's rows (L2 before:

891
00:58:03.999 --> 00:58:05.301
2 shared DBs).

892
00:58:05.581 --> 00:58:06.306
Fix:

893
00:58:06.586 --> 00:58:10.981
Database per service; data via APIs, events or read models.

894
00:58:11.261 --> 00:58:11.965
2.

895
00:58:12.245 --> 00:58:14.698
Synchronous callbacks (A, then B, then A).

896
00:58:16.048 --> 00:58:28.251
Here is the Java code. before: payment-service calls order-service back during capture, then a cycle, and cascading timeouts. after: payment publishes a fact; the order saga reacts.

897
00:58:28.531 --> 00:58:30.259
No call back, no cycle.

898
00:58:33.109 --> 00:58:33.855
3.

899
00:58:34.135 --> 00:58:36.951
Sequential composition of independent calls.

900
00:58:37.231 --> 00:58:38.042
Symptom:

901
00:58:38.322 --> 00:58:45.661
Page latency = sum of calls (L4 B: p50 79 ms versus 31 ms parallel).

902
00:58:45.941 --> 00:58:46.666
Fix:

903
00:58:46.946 --> 00:58:49.101
Parallel calls with per-call timeouts.

904
00:58:49.381 --> 00:58:50.085
4.

905
00:58:50.365 --> 00:58:52.178
No budget for optional data.

906
00:58:52.458 --> 00:58:53.269
Symptom:

907
00:58:53.549 --> 00:58:58.754
The page p99 equals the slowest optional dependency (~2 s).

908
00:58:59.034 --> 00:58:59.759
Fix:

909
00:59:00.039 --> 00:59:08.231
Classify fields as required or optional, and budget and degrade the optional ones (p99 251 ms).

910
00:59:08.511 --> 00:59:09.279
5.

911
00:59:09.559 --> 00:59:12.290
Static instance lists or long-cached DNS.

912
00:59:12.570 --> 00:59:13.381
Symptom:

913
00:59:13.661 --> 00:59:16.754
Errors for minutes after an instance dies (L4 A:

914
00:59:17.034 --> 00:59:18.463
833).

915
00:59:18.743 --> 00:59:19.469
Fix:

916
00:59:19.749 --> 00:59:26.554
Platform discovery with health checks, a short TTL, and a retry on another instance for idempotent calls.

917
00:59:27.904 --> 00:59:28.651
6.

918
00:59:28.931 --> 00:59:31.747
A shared common-model library across services.

919
00:59:32.027 --> 00:59:32.837
Symptom:

920
00:59:33.117 --> 00:59:37.981
Changing one DTO forces every service to redeploy (lock-step releases).

921
00:59:38.261 --> 00:59:38.987
Fix:

922
00:59:39.267 --> 00:59:43.149
Share only technical libraries (logging, tracing, the outbox).

923
00:59:43.429 --> 00:59:48.037
Each service owns its API models, and contracts are specs, not jars.

924
00:59:48.317 --> 00:59:49.064
7.

925
00:59:49.344 --> 00:59:52.224
Nanoservices (one per entity or function).

926
00:59:52.504 --> 00:59:53.315
Symptom:

927
00:59:53.595 --> 00:59:57.520
Dozens of calls per request, and teams owning 10 plus services.

928
00:59:57.800 --> 00:59:58.525
Fix:

929
00:59:58.805 --> 01:00:00.277
Split by capability.

930
01:00:00.557 --> 01:00:02.904
Merge services that always change together.

931
01:00:04.254 --> 01:00:04.937
8.

932
01:00:05.217 --> 01:00:07.350
Big-bang rewrite of the legacy system.

933
01:00:07.630 --> 01:00:08.441
Symptom:

934
01:00:08.721 --> 01:00:12.582
A multi-year project that never catches up with the moving legacy.

935
01:00:12.862 --> 01:00:13.587
Fix:

936
01:00:13.867 --> 01:00:17.771
A strangler fig, slice by slice, with shadow diffs (L4 C:

937
01:00:18.051 --> 01:00:20.398
468 mismatches caught).

938
01:00:20.678 --> 01:00:21.425
9.

939
01:00:21.705 --> 01:00:24.649
Microservices before the domain is understood.

940
01:00:24.929 --> 01:00:25.739
Symptom:

941
01:00:26.019 --> 01:00:29.753
Boundaries in the wrong places, constant cross-service changes.

942
01:00:30.033 --> 01:00:30.758
Fix:

943
01:00:31.038 --> 01:00:38.633
A modular monolith first, with enforced boundaries (L3), extracting services when boundaries have proven stable.

944
01:00:39.983 --> 01:00:40.687
10.

945
01:00:40.967 --> 01:00:43.335
Boundaries enforced only by convention.

946
01:00:43.615 --> 01:00:44.425
Symptom:

947
01:00:44.705 --> 01:00:50.103
Just this once imports of another module's internals, which erode the architecture within a year.

948
01:00:50.383 --> 01:00:51.108
Fix:

949
01:00:51.388 --> 01:00:57.809
Compiler or JPMS, Spring Modulith verify(), ArchUnit or fitness functions in CI.

950
01:00:58.089 --> 01:01:00.713
L3's three violations never compile.

951
01:01:02.063 --> 01:01:02.938
Part 8.

952
01:01:03.218 --> 01:01:04.562
Interview Q and A.

953
01:01:05.812 --> 01:01:06.644
Beginner.

954
01:01:06.924 --> 01:01:07.948
Question 1.

955
01:01:08.228 --> 01:01:11.279
What are microservices, and why would a company use them?

956
01:01:11.559 --> 01:01:12.924
Thirty-second answer:

957
01:01:13.204 --> 01:01:23.401
An architecture where the system is split into small services, each owning a business capability and its data, deployed independently and communicating over the network.

958
01:01:23.681 --> 01:01:33.153
The main reason is organizational: many teams can change and release their parts without coordinating, and services can scale and fail independently.

959
01:01:34.503 --> 01:01:35.506
Question 2.

960
01:01:35.786 --> 01:01:38.261
What does 'database per service' mean, and why?

961
01:01:38.541 --> 01:01:39.906
Thirty-second answer:

962
01:01:40.186 --> 01:01:44.901
Each service owns its tables, and no other service reads or writes them directly.

963
01:01:45.181 --> 01:01:47.698
Others go through its API or its events.

964
01:01:47.978 --> 01:01:55.594
It keeps schema changes local, lets the owner enforce invariants, and prevents one service's load from locking another's data.

965
01:01:56.944 --> 01:01:57.989
Question 3.

966
01:01:58.269 --> 01:02:00.061
What is a distributed monolith?

967
01:02:00.341 --> 01:02:01.707
Thirty-second answer:

968
01:02:01.987 --> 01:02:14.317
A system split into services that still can't be changed or deployed independently, because of shared databases, synchronous call chains and cycles, shared model libraries, or lock-step releases.

969
01:02:14.597 --> 01:02:18.245
It has all the costs of microservices and none of the benefits.

970
01:02:19.595 --> 01:02:20.662
Question 4.

971
01:02:20.942 --> 01:02:22.627
What is a modular monolith?

972
01:02:22.907 --> 01:02:24.273
Thirty-second answer:

973
01:02:24.553 --> 01:02:36.435
A single deployable application divided into modules with enforced boundaries: each module owns its data and exposes a small API, and the others can't reach its internals.

974
01:02:36.715 --> 01:02:41.835
It keeps the simplicity of one deployment while preparing clean seams for extraction later.

975
01:02:43.185 --> 01:02:44.188
Intermediate.

976
01:02:44.468 --> 01:02:45.577
Question 5.

977
01:02:45.857 --> 01:02:47.820
How do you decide service boundaries?

978
01:02:48.100 --> 01:02:49.465
Thirty-second answer:

979
01:02:49.745 --> 01:02:59.708
By business capability and bounded context: what changes together, who owns the data and its invariants, where the language is consistent, and which team will own it.

980
01:02:59.988 --> 01:03:05.961
Check that typical features touch one service and that invariants don't need cross-service transactions.

981
01:03:07.311 --> 01:03:08.421
Question 6.

982
01:03:08.701 --> 01:03:10.599
How do services find each other?

983
01:03:10.879 --> 01:03:12.245
Thirty-second answer:

984
01:03:12.525 --> 01:03:14.018
Through service discovery.

985
01:03:14.298 --> 01:03:24.773
Either the platform does it (Kubernetes Services with DNS and endpoints tracking ready pods), or clients query a registry (Eureka, Consul) and load-balance.

986
01:03:25.053 --> 01:03:31.751
Instances must be removed quickly when unhealthy, and callers retry idempotent requests on another instance.

987
01:03:33.101 --> 01:03:34.189
Question 7.

988
01:03:34.469 --> 01:03:37.989
How do you build a screen that needs data from several services?

989
01:03:38.269 --> 01:03:39.635
Thirty-second answer:

990
01:03:39.915 --> 01:03:53.099
Compose in a BFF or gateway: call the services in parallel, give each a timeout, mark optional parts with a latency budget, and return partial results with a clear unavailable state for optional parts.

991
01:03:53.379 --> 01:03:59.587
For frequently used combined views with filtering or sorting, build a CQRS read model from events.

992
01:04:00.937 --> 01:04:01.961
Question 8.

993
01:04:02.241 --> 01:04:05.633
How do you handle configuration across 26 services?

994
01:04:05.913 --> 01:04:07.278
Thirty-second answer:

995
01:04:07.558 --> 01:04:18.587
Externalize it: environment variables or ConfigMaps for static config, a secrets manager for credentials, a versioned config or flag store for dynamic values.

996
01:04:18.867 --> 01:04:21.406
One artifact runs in every environment.

997
01:04:21.686 --> 01:04:26.913
Treat config changes like deployments: validated, staged, audited, reversible.

998
01:04:28.263 --> 01:04:29.329
Question 9.

999
01:04:29.609 --> 01:04:31.316
What's the strangler fig pattern?

1000
01:04:31.596 --> 01:04:32.961
Thirty-second answer:

1001
01:04:33.241 --> 01:04:35.567
Replace a legacy system incrementally.

1002
01:04:35.847 --> 01:04:49.585
Put a routing facade in front, build the new implementation of one slice, mirror traffic to compare outputs (shadow), send a small share of real traffic (canary), then route everything and retire the legacy slice.

1003
01:04:49.865 --> 01:04:51.337
Repeat slice by slice.

1004
01:04:52.687 --> 01:04:53.455
Senior.

1005
01:04:53.735 --> 01:04:54.781
Question 10.

1006
01:04:55.061 --> 01:04:58.709
How do you prevent cascading failures in a microservice graph?

1007
01:04:58.989 --> 01:05:00.354
Thirty-second answer:

1008
01:05:00.634 --> 01:05:19.173
Keep the synchronous graph small (no cycles, shallow depth, few dependencies per request), put timeouts on every call, isolate resources per dependency (bulkheads), use circuit breakers and load shedding, degrade optional features, and move facts to events so failures don't propagate synchronously.

1009
01:05:20.523 --> 01:05:21.653
Question 11.

1010
01:05:21.933 --> 01:05:24.835
How do you detect and fix a distributed monolith?

1011
01:05:25.115 --> 01:05:26.480
Thirty-second answer:

1012
01:05:26.760 --> 01:05:37.683
Measure it: % of releases touching more than one service, shared tables, synchronous cycles and depth from traces, calls per request, and services with high synchronous fan-in.

1013
01:05:37.963 --> 01:05:48.992
Then fix the worst coupling first: split shared databases, replace callbacks with events, merge services that always change together, and add fitness functions so it doesn't regress.

1014
01:05:50.342 --> 01:05:51.430
Question 12.

1015
01:05:51.710 --> 01:05:55.465
How do you enforce module boundaries inside a Spring Boot service?

1016
01:05:55.745 --> 01:05:57.110
Thirty-second answer:

1017
01:05:57.390 --> 01:06:09.678
Spring Modulith (ApplicationModules.verify() in a test) treats top-level packages as modules and fails the build on access to another module's internal packages or on cycles.

1018
01:06:09.958 --> 01:06:15.313
ArchUnit rules are an alternative, and JPMS enforces it at the compiler level.

1019
01:06:15.593 --> 01:06:20.179
Cross-module reactions use in-process events with transactional delivery.

1020
01:06:21.529 --> 01:06:22.809
Question 13.

1021
01:06:23.089 --> 01:06:26.183
What changes when you extract a module into a service?

1022
01:06:26.463 --> 01:06:27.828
Thirty-second answer:

1023
01:06:28.108 --> 01:06:47.692
Method calls become network calls (timeouts, retries, versioning), in-process events become broker events (outbox, idempotent consumers), the shared database schema moves to its own database (data migration, CDC during transition), and deployment, monitoring and on-call become separate.

1024
01:06:47.972 --> 01:06:50.959
Transactions across the boundary become sagas.

1025
01:06:52.309 --> 01:06:53.503
Question 14.

1026
01:06:53.783 --> 01:06:58.797
How do you keep 26 services consistent without a shared library that couples them?

1027
01:06:59.077 --> 01:07:00.442
Thirty-second answer:

1028
01:07:00.722 --> 01:07:22.567
A golden-path service template and a platform: a generated project structure (four layers, config, health, logging, tracing, security, outbox or inbox), shared technical libraries only (versioned, backward compatible, updated by automation), and contract specs (OpenAPI or AsyncAPI) instead of shared model jars.

1029
01:07:23.917 --> 01:07:24.813
Architect.

1030
01:07:25.093 --> 01:07:26.288
Question 15.

1031
01:07:26.568 --> 01:07:30.707
Modular monolith or microservices for a new grocery startup?

1032
01:07:30.987 --> 01:07:32.352
Thirty-second answer:

1033
01:07:32.632 --> 01:07:42.744
Start with a modular monolith (Spring Boot + Spring Modulith, one database with a schema per module, in-process events) while the domain and team are small.

1034
01:07:43.024 --> 01:07:44.795
Enforce boundaries from day one.

1035
01:07:45.075 --> 01:07:54.845
Extract services when team count, scaling differences (search, tracking) or isolation (payments or PCI) justify the fixed cost per service.

1036
01:07:56.195 --> 01:07:57.390
Question 16.

1037
01:07:57.670 --> 01:08:00.422
How does Conway's law affect your service design?

1038
01:08:00.702 --> 01:08:02.067
Thirty-second answer:

1039
01:08:02.347 --> 01:08:05.761
Systems end up mirroring team communication structures.

1040
01:08:06.041 --> 01:08:16.089
So design teams and services together: stream-aligned teams own services end to end, boundaries follow teams, and a platform team provides the paved road.

1041
01:08:16.369 --> 01:08:23.430
If the architecture you want doesn't match the org, change the org (inverse Conway) or the architecture will drift back.

1042
01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:26.124
Question 17.

1043
01:08:26.404 --> 01:08:30.308
Plan the migration of the legacy admin monolith to services.

1044
01:08:30.588 --> 01:08:31.953
Thirty-second answer:

1045
01:08:32.233 --> 01:08:34.943
Put the gateway in front as the strangler facade.

1046
01:08:35.223 --> 01:08:42.412
Map capabilities and pick slices by value and low entanglement (order history, reports, then catalog editing).

1047
01:08:42.692 --> 01:08:51.780
For each: feed data via CDC, build the service with an anti-corruption layer, shadow and diff, canary, cut over, delete the legacy code.

1048
01:08:52.060 --> 01:08:53.297
Move writes last.

1049
01:08:53.577 --> 01:08:56.969
Track progress by traffic share and deleted legacy code.

1050
01:08:58.319 --> 01:08:59.493
Question 18.

1051
01:08:59.773 --> 01:09:03.847
How would you set SLOs for a checkout that depends on 6 services?

1052
01:09:04.127 --> 01:09:05.493
Thirty-second answer:

1053
01:09:05.773 --> 01:09:26.103
Start from the user journey SLO (checkout success ≥ 99.9 %, p99 at most 1.5 s) and derive dependency SLOs: with 6 required synchronous dependencies, each needs better than 99.98 % unless calls are retried or have fallbacks.

1054
01:09:26.383 --> 01:09:32.506
Budget latency across the parallel calls, and error-budget-alert on the journey, not just per service.

1055
01:09:33.856 --> 01:09:35.093
Question 19.

1056
01:09:35.373 --> 01:09:38.104
When would you merge microservices back together?

1057
01:09:38.384 --> 01:09:39.749
Thirty-second answer:

1058
01:09:40.029 --> 01:09:53.725
When two services always change and deploy together, share data or call each other synchronously in both directions, the split creates sagas for what should be a local transaction, or one team owns both with high cognitive load.

1059
01:09:54.005 --> 01:09:59.509
Merging (into modules of one service) removes network costs and restores transactions.

1060
01:10:00.859 --> 01:10:01.969
Question 20.

1061
01:10:02.249 --> 01:10:06.878
What does the platform team need to provide for 26 services to be viable?

1062
01:10:07.158 --> 01:10:08.523
Thirty-second answer:

1063
01:10:08.803 --> 01:10:32.483
A paved road: a service template, CI or CD with progressive delivery, environment provisioning, discovery and config, observability (tracing, metrics, logs, dashboards by default), security (mTLS, secrets, identity), messaging with outbox or inbox libraries, a service catalog with ownership, fitness-function checks, and documentation and on-call tooling.

1064
01:10:33.833 --> 01:10:34.729
Part 9.

1065
01:10:35.009 --> 01:10:36.417
Summary and Practice.

1066
01:10:37.667 --> 01:10:38.755
Key takeaways.

1067
01:10:39.035 --> 01:10:43.430
Microservices are for independent deployability and team autonomy.

1068
01:10:43.710 --> 01:10:46.099
Without those you have a distributed monolith.

1069
01:10:46.379 --> 01:10:51.137
Synchronous dependencies multiply unavailability and add latency (L1:

1070
01:10:51.417 --> 01:10:58.841
12 in series, then 98.81 %; 8 sequential, then p50 178 ms).

1071
01:10:59.121 --> 01:11:05.094
Split by business capability or bounded context, coarser rather than finer, aligned with teams.

1072
01:11:05.374 --> 01:11:06.974
One database per service.

1073
01:11:07.254 --> 01:11:13.227
Queries across services use composition or read models, and updates across services use sagas.

1074
01:11:14.577 --> 01:11:16.732
Facts as events, commands as calls.

1075
01:11:17.012 --> 01:11:20.575
No synchronous cycles, shallow depth, timeouts everywhere.

1076
01:11:20.855 --> 01:11:23.607
Fitness functions keep the graph healthy (L2:

1077
01:11:23.887 --> 01:11:28.601
FAIL, then PASS: cycles, depth, shared DBs, dependencies per request).

1078
01:11:28.881 --> 01:11:35.537
Modular monolith first, with boundaries the compiler enforces (L3), and extraction when justified.

1079
01:11:35.817 --> 01:11:41.897
Discovery must evict dead instances fast, and callers retry idempotent requests (L4:

1080
01:11:42.177 --> 01:11:44.737
833, then 0 failures).

1081
01:11:46.087 --> 01:11:54.706
Compose in parallel with budgets for optional parts (L4: p99 2,054, then 251 ms).

1082
01:11:54.986 --> 01:11:59.765
Migrate with a strangler fig: shadow, diff, canary, cut over, delete (L4:

1083
01:12:00.045 --> 01:12:03.266
468 mismatches caught before users).

1084
01:12:04.616 --> 01:12:05.939
Decision cheat sheet.

1085
01:12:06.219 --> 01:12:07.563
Here is a quick table:

1086
